// Host-specific niri output configuration for `pi`.
// Deployed to /etc/niri/host.kdl and pulled in by the shared config via
// `include optional=true`.
//
// Outputs are matched on their EDID make/model/serial string rather than on
// the connector name (HDMI-A-1). Both the desk monitor and the Xreal glasses
// plug into that same single HDMI port, so keying on the connector would apply
// one display's settings to the other. Matching on identity means each display
// gets its own block and whichever is plugged in wins.
//
// To find the string for a new display, plug it in and run: niri msg outputs

output "PNP(AOC) 2243W AQXC59A000725" {
    mode "1920x1080@60.000"
    scale 1
    transform "normal"
    position x=0 y=0
}

// XREAL One AR glasses. Same HDMI port as the monitor above — only one can be
// connected at a time — which is exactly why these blocks are keyed on EDID
// identity rather than on the connector both of them present as (HDMI-A-1).
//
// 90 Hz is the panel's preferred mode and the one it negotiates on its own.
// 120 Hz is also offered and is usually worth having on head-mounted displays,
// where a higher refresh rate cuts the judder you notice when turning your
// head. It is not the default here because it is unverified that the Pi 4's
// HDMI will actually drive 1080p120. To try it without committing:
//
//     niri msg output "Nreal XREAL One Unknown" mode 1920x1080@120.000
//
// Scale is 1 because that is what the glasses have been used at. Note that the
// EDID reports a physical size of 1860x1050 mm — nearly two metres wide, which
// is nonsense for a headset — so niri cannot infer a sensible scale and falls
// back to 1. If text reads too small through the optics, try `scale 1.25` or
// `1.5` live with `niri msg output ... scale 1.25` and put whichever works
// here. niri msg changes are temporary and never written to disk, so they are
// safe to experiment with.
output "Nreal XREAL One Unknown" {
    mode "1920x1080@90.000"
    scale 1
    transform "normal"
    position x=0 y=0
}
